concepts.content:
A radiance field is a representation of part of the plenoptic function, based on the idea that the optical content of a scene can be modeled as a cloud of colorful particles with different levels of transparency. Such a representation can be rendered into images using volume rendering. We define a radiance field $L$ as a mapping from the coordinates in a scene to color and density (i.e., transparency) values of the scene content at those coordinates. The coordinates can be 2D ($X,Y$) or 3D ($X,Y,Z$) and may also contain angular dimensions ($\psi$ and $\phi$) representing the viewing angle. Representing color using $r,g,b$ values and density with a scalar $\sigma$, a radiance field is a function:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b,\sigma \quad\quad\triangleleft \quad\text{radiance field}
\end{aligned}$$

The angular coordinates allow an object's color to vary depending on the viewing angle, which is essential for shiny surfaces. We use $L^c$ and $L^\sigma$ to denote the subcomponents that output color and density respectively:
$$\begin{aligned}
    L^c&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b\\
    L^{\sigma}&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow\sigma
\end{aligned}$$

